= Designer Notes

The following chapters cover the unabridged origin story of Telemetry Troubles; everything from humble beginnings to the spectacular boost into outer space.

This section will be compelling for anyone who wants a glimpse into the backstage of game design and development - how I was inspired and motivated, what unexpected trials were waiting for me in the Fog of Design & Development. Facts and artifacts, rumors, conspiracies, industry insights, and strong opinions - that, and more, will wait for you below.



== Programming Games Origins

Despite obscurity, the programming games are a very ancient genre going back to the 60s and the age of mainframes.

The first known instance could be Darwin, invented in August 1961 at Bell Labs by Victor A. Vyssotsky, Robert Morris Sr., and M. Douglas McIlroy (later famous for involvement in early UNIX). It was implemented on an IBM 7090 mainframe.

In Darwin, each player creates a program in 7090 assembly and loads it into the memory area called "arena". These programs run against each other by probing memory, copying themselves, and killing other programs. And the last running program is declared a winner.

Darwin, along with the idea of self-replicating programs like Creeper and Reaper, inspired another programming game named Core War. It defines RedCode, an abstract assembly language for writing battle programs. The code runs on a virtual machine called MARS (stands for Memory Array Redcode Simulator) and is usually accompanied by a graphical memory monitor that allows you to see how programs are moved through memory and eliminate each other.

After exploring the idea of abstract fighting programs, the genre branched into the programming of fighting vehicles. Like RobotWars, developed in 70s for the PLATO system and later ported to other platforms, where you program a robot to battle other robots. Another classic is Omega by Origin, released in 1989, where you design and program battle tanks.

A very interesting variety of programming games evolved with the appearance of 3D-capable gaming consoles. Carnage Heart, created by a Japanese corporation Artdink in 1995, was a very different experience, allowing players to design sophisticated battle-mechs and program them in a visual programming environment that works only with a game controller (no keyboard!) Not only does it involve engaging 3D battles between giant robots, but also a backstory, a well-developed plot, advancing research and development, manufacturing, and strategic elements. It was followed by a bunch of sequels and similar games like Pandora Project (PlayStation, 1996), Marionette Handler (Sega Dreamcast, 1999), Gladiabots (Steam, 2019), and some entries in the Armored Core series.

Then, there were hacking simulations of various complexities. Some were simple mechanical hacking simulations with no programming whatsoever - like epic Uplink by Introversion. But some involved programming elements.

And finally, there are many puzzle games, where you have to write code in order to progress forward. It could be actual code or logic blocks you need to place in the right order.

One of the best examples with immense educational value is ColoBot, where you use a Java-like object-oriented language to program bots to complete multiple missions of planet colonisation. You program bots to find, collect, and process resources, do manufacturing, and fight threatening alien lifeforms.

There is one company (or rather, a one-man company) that is famous for programming elements in its games. That is legendary Zachtronics, with titles like TIS-100 and Shenzhen I/O and ExaPunks, where gameplay is driven by programming.

 

== My Journey

This chapter explores my unexpected journey into the world of programming games.

I have always been fascinated by programming games.

Ever since I started my programming odyssey on the ZX Spectrum in the early 90s, I have been fascinated by the idea of automation. Sure thing, I played a lot of games back then. And subconsciously, I split the games not by genres, but by the level of abstraction.

On level 1, there are games where you directly control the hero. These were arcade-style action games, adventures, platformers, vehicle simulations, and later FPS. The next level holds the games with indirect control - tactics, strategies, tycoons, various simulations, and God-games. You give orders and see the results.

The third level is the most abstract one - you automate units or processes by defining sequences or rules.

My first encounter with that abstraction level was even earlier than I discovered programming - probably when I was 4 or 5. And not with computer games, but with toys - I had a Lunokhod toy, which in turn was a Soviet clone of the American BigTrak programmable six-wheel Sci-Fi tank (ironic that the country that landed an actual Lunokhod robotic rover on the Moon never managed to come up with a viable kids' toy and resorted to cloning one from the ideological enemy). But despite its questionable origin, Lunokhod was an awesome toy. You can program a sequence of actions - move 4 steps forward, turn right, move 2 steps, shoot the laser gun - and see it play out right in front of you.

Then, there were computer games with hints of automation. ZX-Spectrum has strategy games like Nether Earth and Carrier Command. And even though the proper automation was not possible there and you have to give direct orders to your robots and vehicles, I entertained the idea "wouldn't it be cool" if only one could set a sequence of steps, preferebly with some sensor-based conditions.

Later on, I discovered a little obscure DOS game by Gamos called Snake Battle, where you pit your snake against the others in an epic autobattle. And there is an interesting twist - you have to define your snake behavior in a sequence of cards, each describing a pattern or a "situation" and a direction you have to turn when the pattern is matched. Each step, every snake will look through its cards, trying to match the current situation - a wall in front of it or an enemy tail - and figure out the next move.

Later, my interest was reinforced with other titles I've discovered.
I played many of them, explored their designs, and discussed those games with fellow game designers\...
Who can forget programming bots to fight against evil spiders in ColoBot?

Realizing a still unexplored educational value in such games, I made multiple attempts to design and prototype games that would include some elements of programming puzzles or automation.

I used programming game challenges in many of my classes and bootcamps. And I noticed that it is somewhat hard to design a well-balanced and challenging programming game, but when done right and applied properly, it can give an unbelievable boost to the learning flow and general engagement. Gamification in education is not a gimmick, but a really powerful tool. Games, after all, appeared way before modern humans did. They are observed with all advanced species and are widely used to learn valuable life skills in a controlled environment.

Some of my programming games were created with a particular theme and for a particular game jam. Like Sonado, created for Meta Game Jam. It explores the idea of a virtual game console and implements a virtual machine that runs right from the cart image. The same image contains all maps and sprites for the game, along with instructions for a virtual machine specially developed for this particular game (just another of my virtual machines).

I also designed games for particular topics of my development bootcamps. These were very specific games, like a UNIX quest designed to familiarize developers with the shell tools and teach them the UNIX-Way of doing things.

So, from my personal perspective, Telemetry Troubles is nothing new, but another entry in a sequence of programming games I've been designing and developing.



== Ludum Dare 59 - Signal

Ludum Dare 59 was a total failure.

I was not planning to participate in the first place, and no one from the team was able to join. Also, to enter a jam as intense as Ludum Dare without a warm-up is a recipe for disaster.

I failed to warm up due to a ballistic missile attack on my city two days prior that left 60 people injured and 4 dead. A loud and sleepless night destroyed my focus and killed any desire to do anything for the next couple of days.

So how did I end up jamming?

Well, I woke up on Saturday and opened the Ludum Dare site to check the announced theme. Most of the time, I struggle with jam themes. It was always challenging to come up with a well-defined game concept in a limited time. Not this time, though.

"Signal" was simple, clear, very narrow... In fact, so confined that a game idea almost instantly popped up in my mind. No research. No Pinterest surfing for inspiration. No brainstorming... No usual struggle, long considerations, doubts...

Just a pure game concept I can clearly visualize.

There is a deep-space probe at the edge of the solar system. It is on an important mission - maybe searching for rare metal deposits in the Kuiper belt. A lot of money was spent on the design, manufacturing, and launch of the probe. And many years have passed to reach the edge of the solar system.

Once the probe was out and ready to activate and perform its primary mission, a slight problem was discovered. Turns out someone mixed up metric and imperial units while building its Radioisotope Thermoelectric Generators (RTGs). Now the probe doesn't have sufficient power and must be remotely reprogrammed to compensate for the loss and still perform the job.

On the right side of the screen, you would see a wireframe model of a probe resembling Voyager or maybe Cassini-Huygens. Around it, you can see the telemetry stream - some gauges and indicators jumping and blinking, showing all sorts of activities - indicating that the probe was still alive.

On the left side, there would be two windows.

The top one shows emails from the HQ providing updates and requesting activation or deactivation of certain systems.

There is another window at the bottom left, where you can define necessary instructions in a low-level assembly-like language and upload them to the probe. The program will execute, and the desired effect will be matched against the requested requirements. If matched, the associated payment will be deposited into your account.

The space mission you are a part of has a defined burn rate. The goal is to keep the mission alive as long as possible by completing experiments and earning enough money for the next burn.

The mission will be cancelled when you run out of money. It is game over.

That was the game vision. It clearly stuck in my mind.

The only problem was that it was too ambitious even for a team jam. And for a solo developer, the task was insurmountable. I had to design the UI layout and style, develop the inbox, the email viewer and email subsystem, the mission subsystem to issue and check the experiments, the budgeting subsystem to keep the balance and burn cash, the programming UI... And most importantly, I had to develop a simulation for the probe and its subsystems, mirror those instruments on visual telemetry gadgets, and create a virtual machine to control it all.

And only when all these systems and components are ready, can I start implementing actual puzzles - the programming challenges for the probe and its on-board instruments.

Needless to say, by the end of the Jam, I only managed to mock up the necessary systems and create some plausible and almost functioning UI layout. But beyond that, nothing was even remotely playable.

The idea, thought, persisted in my agitated brain. For weeks, I was haunted by the vision of the probe telemetry pulsing and blinking, waiting for assembly instructions to be executed. And once I click the Play button, the program counter will step over my instructions, enable required instruments, and turn on the tape recorder to capture the data stream. That vision was exhilarating.

Probably, I was heavily inspired by the 2024 Voyager 1 remote fix story, when the spacecraft started to return garbage instead of a valid telemetry stream and had to be remotely debugged and reprogrammed. And by "remotely", I mean more than 24 billion kilometers or 162 AU of space between Earth and the probe. Even light takes almost two days to travel there and back.


== Noise Jam

Since Noise Jam doesn't require building everything from scratch and allows you to start early, I've decided to join Noise Jam and fully develop that deep-space probe debugging idea. I considered 3 weeks of Noise Jam to be enough to properly explore and develop the idea.

I was wrong. I established an overambitious scope for the jam - not only was I developing the game with a unique GUI system, but I was also debugging in-game text-mode interface, porting a js13k procedural modeler to Collider.JAM with its compiler and interpreter, writing a software 3D wireframe renderer for authentic probe telemetry, modeling the probe in my own DSL, and also inventing another programming language suitable for the probe programming within the existing game interface (I wanted to avoid using the keyboard if possible, so you can program just with the mouse or a gamepad).

It was another failure. Well, I've managed to ship something barely playable this time, but I discovered that programming puzzles are really hard to come up with, design, playtest, and iterate over. Especially when these puzzles are chained together in a logical sequence (for example, you can't do the loop mission without discovering and unlocking the SUB instruction).

But at least I've achieved something - an actual little sequence of programming puzzles manipulating telemetry and good visual feedback, which I always wanted to have, but never really knew if that would work.

Assembly programming is the most frustrating form of programming due to its low-level nature. You have to do a lot of bits and bytes manipulation, pointer arithmetic, and jumps, often without immediate visual feedback. And that makes assembly programming both frustrating and mysterious. A lot of stuff needs to be done in order to get even minimal results.

Telemetry Troubles is somewhat unique in the way it provides feedback. A running DUSTY-12 program often causes immediate changes in the telemetry stream so you can see the effects of your instructions right away. And I believe it is the only reasonable way to learn programming - when your feedback is direct and immediate.

That is what  Wally Feurzeig, Cynthia Solomon, and Seymour Papert envisioned while creating the sadly forgotten Logo language - a system with immediate feedback built upon constructivist ideas of learning proposed by a Swiss development psychologist, Jean Piaget. A programmer can command the turtle movements and immediately see the resulting graphics.



== JetPakBoy Challenges

The next challenge is to playtest the existing scenario with JetPakBoy. I want to evaluate the effectiveness of learning the foundations of system programming by solving coding puzzles. I hope that the engaging plot and immediate visual feedback will help to establish the learning flow.











